    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 31 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 31. Remember that effective communication and documentation are crucial when navigating the special education system in New York City. This directory provides contact information and also expert-level guidance on communication protocols and escalation strategies.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 31 (Serving District 31)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>715 Ocean Terrace, Staten Island, NY 10301</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-420-5790">718-420-5790</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9em; color: #777; margin-top: 10px;">
                    <strong>Note:</strong> While an email address is not listed, communication through the NYC Schools Account (NYCSA) is the recommended method. Ensure your NYCSA is active and you are regularly checking for updates. If you're unfamiliar with NYCSA, contact your child's school for assistance in setting it up.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Keep meticulous records of all communication attempts, including dates, times, methods of contact, and the names of individuals contacted. This documentation will be invaluable if you need to file a formal complaint.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window as defined in NYS Part 200.5(a)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This is a critical point of law, often overlooked, that can significantly impact your child's access to timely evaluations and services.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary, even if you feel the conversation was productive. Confirmation in writing is essential.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"Request for Evaluation - [Student Name]"</em>. A clear subject line ensures your email is properly routed and addressed.</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names. This log should detail every interaction, including the method of communication (phone, email, in-person), the date and time, the person you spoke with, and a brief summary of the conversation.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder. This proactive approach ensures your concerns are addressed and prevents unnecessary delays in receiving services for your child.
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Clearly state your request or concern. For example: "Request for initial evaluation for special education services" or "Concern regarding implementation of IEP accommodations."</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal). Use a subject line like: "FOLLOW UP: [Original Subject Line]". Briefly reiterate your original request and mention that you are copying the principal for awareness.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. Find contact information for the District Superintendent on the NYC DOE website. In your email, state that you have attempted to contact the CSE and the principal without success and that you are now seeking assistance from the Superintendent.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or if the district fails to meet its obligations under IDEA (Individuals with Disabilities Education Act) and NYS Part 200 Regulations. A State Complaint is a formal way to notify NYSED that the district is not complying with special education law.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-tips" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 31
    </h2>

    <div class="tip-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(350px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #28a745;">Document Everything</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Maintain a comprehensive record of all interactions with the school and district, including emails, phone calls, meetings, and written correspondence. This documentation is crucial for building your case if disagreements arise.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Create a dedicated file (physical or digital) for all special education-related documents.</li>
                <li>After every meeting, send a follow-up email summarizing the discussion and any agreements made.</li>
                <li>Keep copies of all evaluations, IEPs, and progress reports.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #dc3545;">Know Your Rights</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Part 200 Regulations. Understanding your rights and the school's obligations is essential for effective advocacy.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Review the "Parent's Guide to Special Education" provided by NYSED.</li>
                <li>Attend workshops or training sessions on special education law and advocacy.</li>
                <li>Consider consulting with a special education attorney or advocate.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #ffc107;">Be Proactive and Persistent</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Don't wait for problems to arise before contacting the school or CSE. Be proactive in communicating your concerns and advocating for your child's needs. Persistence is key to ensuring your child receives the appropriate services and support.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Regularly communicate with your child's teachers and service providers.</li>
                <li>Attend all IEP meetings and actively participate in the discussion.</li>
                <li>Don't be afraid to ask questions and challenge decisions that you believe are not in your child's best interest.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #17a2b8;">Build a Strong Team</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
               Collaborate with professionals who can support your child's educational needs, such as therapists, tutors, or educational psychologists. A strong team can provide valuable insights and support your advocacy efforts.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Share relevant information and documentation with your child's support team.</li>
                <li>Encourage communication and collaboration among team members.</li>
                <li>Seek out professionals with expertise in your child's specific disability or learning needs.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #343a40;">Q: What is the first step in getting my child evaluated for special education services in District 31?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
            <strong>A:</strong> The first step is to submit a written request for an evaluation to the CSE (Committee on Special Education). This request should clearly state your concerns about your child's academic or developmental progress and explain why you believe a special education evaluation is necessary. Remember to follow the "Written Notice" rule and keep a copy of your request.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #343a40;">Q: How long does the CSE have to complete the evaluation process?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
            <strong>A:</strong> According to New York State regulations (NYS Part 200.5(a)), the CSE has 60 school days from the date they receive your written consent to conduct the evaluation and hold an IEP (Individualized Education Program) meeting. It is crucial to track this timeline and follow up with the CSE if you have not heard back within a reasonable timeframe.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #343a40;">Q: What if I disagree with the results of the school's evaluation?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
            <strong>A:</strong> If you disagree with the school's evaluation, you have the right to request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense, as stipulated under IDEA. The district must either pay for the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to demonstrate that their evaluation was appropriate. You are entitled to receive the IEE results and have them considered by the CSE.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #343a40;">Q: What should I do if my child's IEP is not being implemented properly?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
            <strong>A:</strong> If you believe that your child's IEP is not being implemented properly, you should first communicate your concerns to your child's teacher and the CSE. Document all instances of non-compliance and request an IEP meeting to address the issues. If the problems persist, you can consider filing a State Complaint with NYSED or pursuing mediation or a due process hearing to enforce the IEP.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #343a40;">Q: Where can I find more information about special education rights and resources in New York City?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
            <strong>A:</strong> You can find more information about special education rights and resources on the NYSED website, the NYC DOE website, and through various parent advocacy organizations. Consider contacting INCLUDEnyc, Advocates for Children of New York, or the special education legal services at your local Legal Aid Society.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

